186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1080 "2-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b28773f-c3b2-5ec9-87f0-8f8e127eca1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343482 #edb/ref 1080 "81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e1d1f28-5484-5c86-a90a-082235f872c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343554 #edb/ref 1080 "27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "965afcff-952c-57f1-8381-d0d2c6cfa333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref